# The RAUC signer's validity window, and the margin the build refuses inside.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (PLAN-078 §3a, §S5). The signer's validity used to be a
# literal in two places that agreed only by coincidence: `-days 3650` in
# pkgs/rauc/gen-dev-keys.sh's mint, and `-days 730` in
# docs/design/release-signing.md §2.1's ceremony block. Two literals that agree
# today are the defect this file exists to prevent -- one of them moves, both
# keep looking right, and the number a reader trusts is whichever one they
# happened to open. The value lives here with its reason beside it, the
# generator mints from it, and build/src/signer-window.test.ts holds the
# ceremony block to it.
#
# Plain `KEY=value` lines only: no logic, no expansion, no command
# substitution -- pkgs/rauc/key-algorithms.env's shape, for its reasons.
#
# COMMITTED, and for key-algorithms.env's reason: meta/ is gitignored and
# generated when absent, so a window declared only there would be absent from a
# fresh checkout, and a generator that finds no declaration falls back to a
# number compiled into itself -- the hardcoded choice this parameter exists to
# remove, reintroduced one level down.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE MAY NOT DECLARE: the DEVICE's verification semantics. The
# window only bounds an attacker because the device compares a signer against
# the current clock, and that property is asserted -- as an absence -- by
# verify/src/checks-rauc.ts's `rauc-keyring-verifies-against-now`. A knob here
# that relaxed it would let one commit shorten the window and disable the thing
# that makes it mean something.

# THE RAUC SIGNER'S VALIDITY, IN DAYS -- meta/rauc/signer.cert.pem, and the
# `-days` of §2.1's signer step.
#
# 45 days, decided 2026-09-04 (PLAN-078 §3a), and it is simultaneously two
# commitments that pull the same way:
#
#   * THE ATTACKER'S USABLE TIME after stealing the signer. There is no CRL
#     path to devices, so a stolen signer is out-waited rather than revoked;
#     45 days against the previous 730 is a factor of sixteen, and those ten
#     days over §3's original 35 are the whole price of the decision below.
#   * THE RELEASE CADENCE BEING COMMITTED TO. A signer that expires with no
#     reissue stops ALL releases: the build refuses (see the threshold below)
#     and rauc's own read-back would fail at the current clock. Under PLAN-078
#     §4a the user chose monthly ROOT access -- no intermediate CA -- so each
#     reissue is a root ceremony, and 45 days against a monthly ceremony leaves
#     about a fortnight of slack. A ceremony that slips a week is then a
#     scheduling problem rather than a publishing outage. 35 days left about
#     five days, which is not enough to convene one at short notice.
#
# It is also far longer than any drift a working clock accumulates, which is
# what keeps PLAN-078 §5's clock-ahead brick reachable only by a grossly wrong
# RTC rather than by ordinary error.
#
# Changing this costs a SIGNER REISSUE, which release-signing.md §2.2 already
# treats as routine: it needs the CA key and no fleet update. It does NOT cost
# a fleet rollover -- devices trust the CA, not the signer.
MOS_RAUC_SIGNER_VALIDITY_DAYS=45

# THE MARGIN THE BUILD REFUSES INSIDE, IN DAYS.
#
# rauc warns -- once it is given a `--keyring`, which build/src/tools/rauc.ts
# now passes -- when a certificate expires "in less than a month". That warning
# is upstream's and its 30 days are not configurable here. This value is the
# second, harder line: inside it the build REFUSES to sign rather than warning,
# on pkgs/rauc/versions.env's precedent of refusing a PENDING hash instead of
# softening it into a message somebody scrolls past.
#
# 7 days, and the arithmetic is the reason. With a 45-day window and a warning
# band that opens at 30, there are about fifteen days of runway. Splitting it
# leaves roughly eight days in which the build still publishes while saying
# loudly that a ceremony is due, and seven in which the calendar has
# demonstrably slipped and a release signed anyway would be a release whose
# signer dies inside the week. A threshold at or above 30 would make every
# warned build a refused build and delete the runway; a threshold of 0 would
# make the refusal fire only once nothing could be published anyway.
#
# It must stay strictly below rauc's own 30 for that reason, and strictly above
# 0 so that the refusal precedes the outage rather than reporting it.
MOS_RAUC_SIGNER_REISSUE_THRESHOLD_DAYS=7
